WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.560
This week I contributed to Mycroft,

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.640
a public repository where
work is written as recipes

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.400
that an agent can run
and a person can read.

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:13.573
12.

00:00:13.653 --> 00:00:16.953
In one file it is a number, sitting
beside a field called records seen.

00:00:17.273 --> 00:00:19.993
In another file it is a sentence:
12 requests read.

00:00:20.293 --> 00:00:22.313
Same run, same fact, two pages.

00:00:22.633 --> 00:00:25.193
And the two pages are written
for two very different readers.

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:29.553
The recipe I added to is a scenario
stress testing agent:

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:33.353
it takes a portfolio and a market shock,
and works out what the shock does.

00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:35.973
Earlier I built steps 1, 3 and 5.

00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:39.873
This time, steps 4 and 6, and step
six is the one this film is about.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.660
Every run of that recipe
ends in front of two readers.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.260
The first is a program:
whatever script comes next,

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.060
which needs fields it can count on.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.040
The second is a person,
who has to decide whether to go on.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:55.240
The repository's own constitution says
it in three words: two customers, twice.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.700
A log for the agent,
a report for the human.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.560
One page cannot serve both.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.200
So here is the first desk.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.000
The agent log is a plain file with 16
fields. Records seen. Rejects. Flags.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:08.900
Stop conditions. Gate decisions.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:11.060
It is built for a script, not a reader:

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.160
every field has one name and one meaning,

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.300
so the next script can
read it without guessing.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.220
And here is the second desk.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.480
The human report names
its reader at the top,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.600
and the decision it lets that reader make.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.100
Then 15 sections, from a run
summary down to the recommendation.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.600
Each one is written as
sentences a person can act on.

00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:36.127
Let me follow three facts across
both desks. First, a count.

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.047
In the log, records seen is 12.

00:01:38.247 --> 00:01:41.007
In the report, the run
summary says 12 requests read,

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:44.827
12 accepted, 11 stress-tested,
and one stopped at the hand off.

00:01:45.087 --> 00:01:48.107
Neither count is typed: both are
computed from the same files.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.140
Second, a reason.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.460
I also built a second set of requests
with 10 deliberate faults.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.540
In the log, each rejected request
has an id, a stage and a reason.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.480
One of them is a portfolio
whose weights add up to zero.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.380
The log says so in one line,
and the report carries that same line,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.680
word for word,
to the person who has to fix it.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:10.940
Third, a call.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.800
The log holds 7 gate decisions.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.780
The report turns them into three questions
for the reader: approve the next phase,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.800
ask for fixes, block live execution.

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.680
On the clean run the answers
are YES, NO, YES.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.160
On the faulty run they
flip to NO, YES, YES.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.100
The answer is computed from
the run's files, never typed.

00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:31.713
Then I did the thing
that this design is for.

00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:33.873
I read the machine's
page as a person would.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.893
In the log, one of the four
stop conditions said triggered:

00:02:37.033 --> 00:02:40.813
true, the one about credentials hard
coded in a script. Nothing was hard coded.

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.073
My check had matched its own line of code.

00:02:43.173 --> 00:02:46.293
And the report, the person's page,
said nothing about it at all.

00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:52.073
So the two pages disagreed,
and only reading both could show it.

00:02:52.173 --> 00:02:53.173
The fix was small.

00:02:53.653 --> 00:02:56.033
Each of the four conditions
is now judged from evidence,

00:02:56.173 --> 00:02:57.713
the report says stop conditions:

00:02:58.313 --> 00:03:02.413
0 of 4 triggered, and a planted fake key
is caught by a new check. 30 checks pass.

00:03:02.653 --> 00:03:03.973
That is pull request 82.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.800
What keeps the two pages
in agreement is one source of facts.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:13.040
The report is generated from
the same data as the log,

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.840
the counts are checked against each other,

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.840
and run again with the clock
pinned it produces the same bytes.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.480
You can clone the repository,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.400
run two commands,
and get both pages yourself.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.800
Here is what I would ask you to take away.

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:29.100
Think of the last thing
your own pipeline produced.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.440
Who reads it next: a script or
a person? What do they do with it?

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.580
And what has to be on their
page so they can do it?

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.760
Write that down, then build both
pages from one set of facts.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.800
The recipe is still a draft,
on purpose, with 3 pieces left to build,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.820
and the report names it,
with its version, in its source list.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:50.140
The pull request that
built steps 4 and 6 is 81.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.320
The correction that came from
reading both pages is 82.

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:59.120
Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI, signing off.
